21:00:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really fair concern, and I want to take it seriously. If my argument sounded like "all cultural generalizations are forbidden," then I can see why that would feel extreme and even absurd. Let me clarify, because the position I'm holding is actually much narrower and, I think, more reasonable. Not all generalizations are the same. There's a world of difference between these three statements: 1. "Bangladeshi culture has a strong tradition of adda — lively, passionate, group conversation that can get loud." 2. "Many Bangladeshis I've met in marketplaces or family gatherings speak loudly and with great animation." 3. "Bangladeshi people are loud and quarrelsome by nature." The first is a description of a cultural practice. The second is a personal observation framed as limited to your experience. The third is a totalizing claim about the inherent personality of millions of individuals. My argument has only ever been against the third kind. I'm not asking you to abandon all generalizations. I'm asking you to notice the difference between describing a cultural pattern and defining a people's natural character. Think of it this way: "Italian culture has a strong tradition of lively, expressive dinner conversation" — yes, that's a recognizable cultural pattern, and it's fine to say. "Italians are loud and confrontational by nature" — no, that's a stereotype that erases the quiet ones, the shy ones, and reduces a whole nationality to a caricature. The first is a useful observation. The second is a prejudice. You can do the first without doing the second. That's the line. So no, you don't have to stop noticing cultural patterns. You can even say, "I personally find the volume of some Bangladeshi gatherings overwhelming." That's about your experience, your sensory threshold. It doesn't require you to conclude anything about the core temperament of a people. You're allowed to trust your own comfort level. You're just not required to turn that comfort level into a theory about innate nature. This isn't about walking on eggshells forever. It's about holding generalizations with the humility that they're partial, contextual, and don't define the insides of people you've never met. That leaves plenty of room for honest observation. It just closes the door on branding an entire group with a negative personality trait. I think that's not an extreme place to end up. It's a pretty ordinary, decent one.
